# Documentation and Specification License

Copyright (c) 2026 Feng Tao (Nate)

This file governs the **non-code** material in the Nef repository: the language
specification, grammar and semantics documents, tutorials, guides, API
reference prose, blog posts, talks and slides, and the README text itself.

## You are free to

- **Share** — copy and redistribute the material in any medium or format.
- **Adapt** — remix, translate, transform, and build upon the material,
  including for commercial purposes (a paid course that teaches Nef using
  excerpts of the specification is allowed).
- Use it freely, with no obligation to release your derivative work under this
  license.

This is **CC BY 4.0** — attribution required, but otherwise no restrictions and
no share-alike obligation. Translations of the specification by third parties
may be published commercially, provided Nef and the author are credited.

## Under the following terms

- **Attribution.** You must give appropriate credit to **Feng Tao (Nate)** as
  the author of the original material, provide a link to the license, and
  indicate if changes were made. You may do so in any reasonable manner, but
  not in any way that suggests the licensor endorses you or your use.

  Suggested attribution line:
  `Adapted from the Nef Language Specification, © 2026 Feng Tao (Nate), CC BY 4.0.`

- **No additional restrictions.** You may not apply legal terms or technological
  measures that restrict others from doing anything the license permits.
  A paid book may still be sold; you just may not use DRM or contract terms to
  prevent buyers from further sharing the portions derived from the spec.

## Notices

- The **name "Nef" and the logo** are not licensed by this document. See
  `TRADEMARK_POLICY.md`. A translated specification must still be titled
  honestly ("An unofficial translation of the Nef Language Specification") and
  must not imply it is the official document.
- The **source code** in this repository is not licensed by this document. It is
  under Apache-2.0 with the Nef Output Exception; see `LICENSE` and
  `LICENSE-EXCEPTION` at the repository root. This covers every program in the
  repository, including the scripts that build and check the documentation site
  (`tools-site/*.mjs` and the `.vitepress/` configuration) and the VS Code
  extension source.
- Code samples, snippets and grammar files reproduced from the documentation are
  dedicated to be free of restriction: you may use any snippet appearing in the
  Nef documentation in your own programs and in any license you choose, without
  attribution, even though the surrounding prose requires attribution.
- The same dedication covers the seven sample programs under `samples/` and the
  program printed in the repository README. It is made by the copyright holder
  and takes precedence over the Apache License for those files, wherever they
  appear in the repository; the operative statement is the last entry of the
  license summary in `NOTICE`.
- The **full text of the CC BY 4.0 license** is at
  https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode

## Why docs and code have different licenses

Code needs a patent grant and an output exception, which only Apache-2.0 gives
cleanly. Prose needs something humans recognise as "you may reuse this, just
say where it came from", which a code license handles badly. Keeping them
separate avoids the common mistake of applying a copyleft or attribution-only
document license to source files, or the opposite mistake of making the
specification look like it governs user programs.
